    "available_at": "2026-11-10T14:01:01.616364+00:00",
    "body": "AI-generated\nBy Alex Lennane\n10 November 2026\nFreightos has launched a new AI-native platform for freight forwarders, as an increasingly bitter dispute between its management and founder Zvi Schreiber over the company’s growth strategy heads towards a shareholder showdown next month.\nDr Schreiber, who founded Freightos in 2012 and resigned from its board in February, has accused the company of abandoning its growth ambitions in favour of profitability, and is campaigning to remove chairman Udo Lange and change the company’s strategic direction.\n“The board calls my plan a ‘sudden strategic pivot’. The opposite is true,” he told The Loadstar .\n“For 14 years, Freightos’ strategy was to maximise growth, with the transactional platform as the primary long-term opportunity. That’s what shareholders invested in.”\nBut chief executive Pablo Pinillos rejected the suggestion that Freightos had to choose between expanding its transaction platform and developing its software business.\n“There is not a real platform-first versus solutions-first idea, because in the end the platform and the solutions reinforce each other,” he told The Loadstar .\nHe argued that improved software, AI-driven decision-making, and automation would generate more bookings, producing additional data that would further improve the platform.\n“Bookings follow,” he said, describing a strategy built around three interconnected layers: intelligence; orchestration; and network connectivity.\nThe new Freightos for Forwarders platform brings together carrier rates, quoting, procurement, booking management, and market intelligence, alongside AI agents designed to convert unstructured customer enquiries into quotations, using forwarders’ own commercial rules and margins.\nFreightos said more than 300 forwarders were already operating on the new platform, although the AI modules and more advanced workflows were still being introduced progressively as customers migrated to the new system.\nThe refreshed interface and live booking functions are operational for migrated customers, with further AI capabilities, integrations, and tailored workflows to follow.\nThe company said it was aiming to reduce quotation turnaround times from hours or days to minutes, and improve quote-to-book conversion rates, but declined to provide projections for additional transactions generated by the launch.\nThe platform connects with more than 75 carriers and the 7LFreight trucking network, while APIs allow forwarders to integrate Freightos’s capabilities with existing transport management systems and proprietary AI tools.\nMr Pinillos said the upgraded technology should help drive growth in both software revenue and transaction volumes.\n“The expectation is that we are able to get more volumes from a transactions perspective,” he said, while making Freightos’s software more embedded in customers’ operations.\nBut Dr Schreiber argues that the company has lost momentum by shifting its focus away from the neutral booking network that he believes represents its principal long-term opportunity.\nIn the second quarter, Freightos reported revenue of $7.7m, up just 3% year on year. Platform revenue increased 19%, to $2.9m, while Solutions revenue fell 4%, to $4.8m.\nDr Schreiber believes AI will increasingly commoditise software products, making the company’s carrier connectivity, transaction infrastructure, and marketplace network more valuable.\nHe wants Freightos to accelerate investment in its platform and relaunch its marketplace for small and medium-sized shippers.\nMr Pinillos said Freightos would continue developing its shipper products, including its enterprise-focused Procure offering and marketplace for smaller customers, although he gave no timetable for a comparable AI-native launch.\nHe also insisted that the company’s push towards profitability was not incompatible with renewed growth.\n“Breakeven is not a strategy, and it’s not the finish line,” he said.\nHe described the breakeven target as a commitment made by previous management, in 2023, arguing that the company had needed greater discipline over investment returns and a cost base that had become too large for its near-term economics.\nAchieving breakeven would allow Freightos to fund future growth from its own resources, he said.\nBut Dr Schreiber disputes the suggestion that more aggressive investment would require additional fundraising. He argued: “Freightos doesn’t need to raise money to grow. It already raised the capital for growth: that’s exactly why we went public, and what shareholders invested in.”\nHe said Freightos had approximately $21m in net cash and no debt, and needed to redirect its spending towards growing the transaction platform.\n“Without aggressive platform growth, where is the upside for shareholders?” he asked.\nMr Pinillos said he expected double-digit revenue growth in the future, although the company would provide specific guidance separately.\nThe dispute is now heading towards a confrontation at Freightos’s annual general meeting on 16 December, where Dr Schreiber is seeking the removal of chairman Udo Lange, annual director elections, and an advisory resolution advocating changes to the company’s strategy.\n“The Freightos board is now so anti-growth that it is engaging in what I can only call blatant vote rigging,” Dr Schreiber alleged.\nHe claims the company has effectively prevented shareholders from supporting his advisory resolution through its normal proxy voting arrangements, leaving attendance at the AGM as the only route to voting in favour.\n“A shareholder who votes through their broker to remove the chairman, or simply to reappoint EY as auditors, will be recorded as voting against growth,” he said.\nThe company’s proxy statement confirms that Proposal 4, Dr Schreiber’s advisory resolution on strategy, has been omitted from its proxy card. It states that even shareholders who submit voting instructions on other resolutions will have their shares voted against Proposal 4.\nFreightos said the decision followed legal advice, with the board arguing that the resolution was inconsistent with shareholders’ rights under applicable law and the company’s articles. It objected particularly to the proposal’s abstract wording and its combination of questions about chairman qualifications and corporate strategy.\nAsked whether shareholders could support the resolution without travelling to Israel, Mr Pinillos told The Loadstar that they would need to attend the AGM, although he suggested online attendance might be possible.\n“It could be online or could be physically,” he said, adding that the company had not yet decided how attendance would be defined for the meeting.\nMr Pinillos also defended the board’s opposition to annual director elections, arguing that the existing staggered structure provided continuity, and that annual elections risked introducing instability into the company’s long-term strategy.\nAsked about departures from Freightos’ management team, he acknowledged that parts of the organisation, including management, needed rebuilding.\nHowever, he declined to engage directly with Dr Schreiber’s criticism of the board.\n“I don’t have anything more than respect for what he has built in this company,” he said. “I’m not focused on who is right or who is wrong.”\nAt the conclusion of the interview, he added: “What I’m not willing to spend a single second of my time on is debating someone else’s opinions.”\nWith Freightos now rolling out its next generation of AI-enabled technology, the question is whether its integrated approach can deliver the renewed growth both sides say they want, without the change of strategy and leadership demanded by its founder.\nCheck out today’s special episode of The Loadstar Podcast , when we team up with Container Trades Statistics (CTS) CEO Nigel Pusey to unpack the latest data and explore the forces shaping global trade",
